Micronix boot loaders, extracted for disassembly
================================================

Two stages boot a Micronix floppy.  The DJ/DMA rom reads 0x80 bytes from
the first sector of track 0 into 0x0080 and jumps there; that first level
boot builds a channel program, reads the second level in, and jumps to
it.  The second level is the one that knows the v6 filesystem: it lists
the root directory, finds a kernel, and loads it.  There is no source for
it, which is why these are here.

micronix16-boot1.bin    128 bytes   org 0x0080  first level
micronix16-loader.bin   9728 bytes  org 0x0100  second level
micronix14-boot1.bin    128 bytes   org 0x0080  first level
micronix14-trk0.bin     3328 bytes              track 0, 26 x 128
micronix14-trk1.bin     7680 bytes              track 1, 15 x 512

1.6, from disks/floppies/Micronix1.6/standalone.bin
---------------------------------------------------

Both addresses come from the first level boot itself, and the loader runs
in the simulator at them, so they are not guesses.

    0080  ld a,26 / ld (0050),a / ld hl,00be / ld (0051),hl
    008f  out (ef),a                      run the channel at 00be
    0091  wait for completion at 00d7
    0098  ld hl,00dd / ld bc,40ff / ld de,0014
    00a1  walk twenty sector statuses, counting bad ones in d
    00b2  ld a,d / or a / jp z,0100       all good: enter the loader
    00bb  jp 00bb                         otherwise hang

The channel reads track 0 to dma ffff00.  With 512 byte sectors that puts
sector 0 off the top of memory and sector 1 at ffff00+200 = 1000100,
which wraps in twenty four bits to 000100 - so the sectors numbered 1
through 9 land at 0100 through 12ff, and the second track read follows at
1300.  Nineteen sectors, contiguous, which is what the file holds.

It disassembles as Whitesmith's C: "ld sp,0100 / call 0a67 / jp 0000" is
crt0 calling main and exiting, and the strings are in there - "Micronix
DJDMA loader", "Drive not ready", "Format?", "Loading", "Entering".

1.41, from disks/dist/UX141_SA.IMD
-----------------------------------

Eight inch, and track 0 is the usual single density boot track: 26
sectors of 128 bytes, with the rest of the disk 15 sectors of 512.  So
the first level boot is exactly one sector.  It is byte for byte the same
block as the 1.3 standalone (disks/boot.IMD), which is worth knowing
before reading too much into either.

Its channel program, at 00dd, decodes as

    23 80 30 00        set dma address 003080
    20 01 01 00 00     read sector cyl 1 sec 1 drive 0
    22 00 00 00 00 00  sense drive status
    25 00              controller halt

    ee: 20 00 03 00 00 read sector cyl 0 sec 3 drive 0
        25 00          controller halt

and the block ends "c3 80 30", jp 3080.  Between the two programs it
reads a sense byte back out of the channel at 00e9, uses it to index a
table of pairs at 00f5 (0134 0134 0229 0222), and pokes what it finds
into the loaded image at 3090 - drive parameters chosen from what the
sense reported.

That accounts for 640 bytes at 3080, and the bytes at 3080 do not look
like an entry point, so either the load is larger than those two commands
suggest or the entry is not at the start.  Both whole tracks are here
rather than a guessed span; disassembling them is how to settle it.

How these were extracted
------------------------

    dumpsec -c 0 -n 1 ../dist/UX141_SA.IMD          >micronix14-trk0.bin
    dumpsec -c 1 -n 1 ../dist/UX141_SA.IMD          >micronix14-trk1.bin
    dumpsec -c 0 -n 1 <a bdev(2,93) name for the 1.6 standalone>

the last being both sides of cylinder 0, whose first sector is the first
level boot and whose remaining nineteen are the second level.

src/tools/dumpsec writes sectors in sector number order through imd_read,
the same path the simulator's DJDMA takes, so a sector in these files is
the sector the controller would fetch.  Note this is not what mnix's
block command gives you: readblk maps a filesystem block through the
driver's interleave, block 0 always reads as zeros, and a 512 byte block
reader cannot address a 128 byte boot track at all.

A raw image needs a bdev(2,minor) name, or a symlink chain ending in one,
because that is where its geometry comes from; an IMD file carries its
own.  Be careful which file such a name points at - the .trim20k images
beside the .bin ones have had the first forty blocks removed, which is
the whole reserved area, so a loader is precisely what is not in them.
